        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "Toronto's offseason has shifted from quiet reflection to aggressive construction, signaling a front office unafraid to bet heavily on the next two years. <strong>The Maple Leafs signed first-overall pick Gavin McKenna to a three-year entry-level contract, locking in the new maximum allowable cap hit of $1.075 million</strong> while securing his future with the club. This move, paired with the two-year deal for defenseman Emil Andrae, confirms a heavy investment in youth despite the team finishing 8th in the Atlantic Division with just 78 points last season. The roster is now reshaped around McKenna's cap hit and Andrae's NHL-ready presence, even as the club sits roughly $2.75 million over the salary cap. With restricted free agents resolved and development camp underway, the focus is clear: <strong>watch how the front office navigates the cap squeeze to finalize a roster built for a deeper Atlantic Division run next year</strong>.",
